#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722227 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=e^t,\\qquad y=e^t\\cos t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThe indicated segment is shown below.\n\n![Parametric curve segment with direction and parameter endpoints](images/38/38d4840253365e13b08d93e0cfcf2194e759cb95d7b7fda9ceaec3805dd5556f.png)\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, use\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=e^t,\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=e^t\\cos t-e^t\\sin t=e^t(\\cos t-\\sin t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the derivatives and the parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_0^1\\sqrt{e^{2t}+e^{2t}(\\cos t-\\sin t)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^1 e^t\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t-2\\sin t\\cos t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^1 e^t\\sqrt{2-\\sin(2t)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $y$ requires the product rule. In simplifying the speed, use $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ and $2\\sin t\\cos t=\\sin(2t)$. Also, $\\sqrt{e^{2t}}=e^t$ because $e^t>0$. Thus the constant inside the final square root is $2$, and the cross term has a minus sign. Leave the integral unevaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722232 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=(t-1)^3+(t-1)^2,\\\\\ny&=(t-1)^3-(t-1)^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ to $t=3$.\n\n![](images/c4/c435bdf976da9c9106c81b7620c90acd80f7f6a446703a173a3cc6a18c258757.png)\n\nChoose an integral expression valid on the entire parameter interval. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, the arc length is\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x'(t)]^2+[y'(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=3(t-1)^2+2(t-1),\\\\\ny'(t)&=3(t-1)^2-2(t-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $(A+B)^2+(A-B)^2=2A^2+2B^2$ to find the squared speed $v(t)^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t)^2\n &=18(t-1)^4+8(t-1)^2\\\\\n &=(t-1)^2\\bigl(18(t-1)^2+8\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the speed is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{[x'(t)]^2+[y'(t)]^2}\n=|t-1|\\sqrt{18(t-1)^2+8}.\n$$\n\nHere $t-1$ is negative for $0\\leq t<1$ and positive for $1<t\\leq3$. Neither $t-1$ nor $1-t$ can replace $|t-1|$ on the entire interval. Therefore,\n\n$$\nL=\\int_0^3|t-1|\\sqrt{18(t-1)^2+8}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAt $t=1$ both derivatives vanish, so the speed is zero there. The continuous nonnegative speed still gives the arc length across that point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722234 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=3t^2+1,\\qquad y=5t-1,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq1?\n$$\n\n![](images/89/89550464608cc7888bc3ec73436c3944252a204954cb0da6c702018910016d65.png)\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, the arc length is\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x'(t)]^2+[y'(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to the parameter:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=6t,\\qquad y'(t)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the derivatives and the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL&=\\int_0^1\\sqrt{(6t)^2+5^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n &=\\int_0^1\\sqrt{36t^2+25}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constants added to the coordinates disappear on differentiation. Use both coordinate derivatives, rather than the slope $y'(t)/x'(t)$, inside the square root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722245 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=t^3+t^2,\\qquad y=t^3-t^2,\\qquad -2\\leq t\\leq-1?\n$$\n\n![](images/b3/b337ea648faf08f3ce4f904f5327193e34d3d273cf06dc1710f3170241bbc5f5.png)\n\nChoose an integral expression. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametric curve, the arc length is\n\n$$\nL=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{[x'(t)]^2+[y'(t)]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst find both derivatives:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2+2t,\\qquad y'(t)=3t^2-2t.\n$$\n\nThe squared speed $v(t)^2$ is the sum of the squared derivatives. The cross terms cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t)^2\n &=(3t^2+2t)^2+(3t^2-2t)^2\\\\\n &=9t^4+12t^3+4t^2+9t^4-12t^3+4t^2\\\\\n &=18t^4+8t^2=t^2(18t^2+8).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking a square root gives $|t|\\sqrt{18t^2+8}$. Since $t<0$ throughout $[-2,-1]$, we have $|t|=-t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{-2}^{-1}(-t)\\sqrt{18t^2+8}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe factor $-t$ is positive on this interval. Replacing $|t|$ by $t$ would give a negative integral, which cannot be this arc length."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722259 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,-\\sqrt{2})$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta-\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the negative $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722260 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{6})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(-\\sqrt{6})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=2\\cdot(2\\sqrt{2})=4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{6} \\\\\ny_B&=4\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2\\sqrt{6},-2\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722264 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\theta)$ and Cartesian coordinates $(0,6)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\theta+\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "The point $A$ lies on the positive $y$-axis, so we may take $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nApply the signed angular offset:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\phi_B&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(3,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\ny_B&=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722266 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(r^{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-3\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36}=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=(6)^2=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(36,\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=36\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-18\\sqrt{2} \\\\\ny_B&=36\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)=36\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=18\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-18\\sqrt{2},18\\sqrt{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722268 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{4\\pi}{3})$ and Cartesian coordinates $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$. The radius $r$ is positive. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\pi)$. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "First, recover the positive radius of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4}=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the radial relationship:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_B&=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot(2)=\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\pi)$. Convert each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-1\\right)=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\pi\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(0\\right)=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{2}{3},0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722277 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $7$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $270^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=7$ and $\\theta=270^{\\circ}$. The point is on the negative $y$-axis. Thus $\\cos(270^{\\circ})=0$ and $\\sin(270^{\\circ})=-1$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(7)\\cos(270^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=(7)(0)\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(7)\\sin(270^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=(7)(-1)\\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(0,-7)$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=0+49=49=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722284 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}=9=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722286 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $3$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\n$(x,y)=(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=3$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. Thus $\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=(3)\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=(3)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}=9=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722287 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $225^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\theta=225^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $3$ with reference angle $45^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $\\sin(225^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cos(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\sin(225^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8},- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{1}{32}=\\frac{1}{16}=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722305 :question/problem "The distance from $P$ to the origin is $\\frac{1}{2}$. The ray from the origin through $P$ makes an angle of $150^{\\circ}$ measured counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "The description gives $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\theta=150^{\\circ}$. The angle is in quadrant $2$ with reference angle $30^{\\circ}$. Thus $\\cos(150^{\\circ})=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin(150^{\\circ})=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nUse $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$. Calculate the horizontal coordinate first, then the vertical coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=r\\cos\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cos(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=r\\sin\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\sin(150^{\\circ})\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian coordinates are $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{4})$. As a check, $x^2+y^2=\\frac{3}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}=r^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722316 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^3+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=8\\cos^3t+\\sin t, & Q&=2\\cos t+\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(8\\cos^3t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad-(2\\cos t+\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=8\\cos^4t+5\\sin t\\cos t+2\\sin^2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\\n&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $2\\sin^2t=1-\\cos(2t)$ and $5\\sin t\\cos t=\\frac52\\sin(2t)$. Substituting and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(4+3\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)+\\frac52\\sin(2t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[4t+\\frac32\\sin(2t)+\\frac14\\sin(4t)-\\frac54\\cos(2t)\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=\\left(8\\pi-\\frac54\\right)-\\left(-\\frac54\\right)=8\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722329 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+2y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=2\\cos t+\\sin t, & Q&=2\\cos t+2\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand and collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(2\\cos t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\\\\n&\\quad-(2\\cos t+2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=2\\cos^2t+5\\sin t\\cos t+4\\sin^2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe squared terms have different coefficients, so their sum is not constant. Use $\\cos^2t=(1+\\cos(2t))/2$, $\\sin^2t=(1-\\cos(2t))/2$, and $\\sin t\\cos t=\\sin(2t)/2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(3-\\cos(2t)+\\frac52\\sin(2t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[3t-\\frac12\\sin(2t)-\\frac54\\cos(2t)\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=\\left(6\\pi-\\frac54\\right)-\\left(-\\frac54\\right)=6\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722331 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,\\mathbf i-(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The parametrization traverses $C$ once counterclockwise, so the outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y'-Q x')\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P$ and $Q$ are evaluated at $(x(t),y(t))$.\n\nAlong $C$, substitute $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=2\\sin t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP&=2\\sin t-2\\cos t, & Q&=-2\\cos t-2\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t, & y'(t)&=2\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two mixed terms cancel. Using $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y'-Q x'&=(2\\sin t-2\\cos t)(2\\cos t)\\\\\n&\\quad-(-2\\cos t-2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\\\\n&=-4\\cos^2t-4\\sin^2t=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\int_0^{2\\pi}-4\\,\\mathrm{d}t=[-4t]_0^{2\\pi}=-8\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe negative sign means that the net flow is inward.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722343 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(11,\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=11\\cos\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=11\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=11\\sin\\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=11\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{11 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722347 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(7,330^{\\circ}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=7\\cos\\left(330^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=7\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=7\\sin\\left(330^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=7\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- \\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2},- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722349 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(4,\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- 2 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)\\\\\n &=4\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- 2 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(- 2 \\sqrt{2},- 2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722355 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(6,\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=6\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=6\\sin\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\\n &=6\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- 3 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(3,- 3 \\sqrt{3}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722365 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(6,135^{\\circ}\\right)$. Find the exact Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, use\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst calculate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\left(135^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=6\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=- 3 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen calculate the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=6\\sin\\left(135^{\\circ}\\right)\\\\\n &=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n &=3 \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates are $\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{2},3 \\sqrt{2}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722373 :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/fd/fdfa1dc87a032577bfd18d266de5fd11dde014b07e1ecdde5bc8626474158e00.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+2x\\,\\mathbf j$ through the curve $C$ parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$, with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nFor increasing $t$, the curve runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$, with tangent direction $(1,2t)$. Its clockwise normal direction is $(2t,-1)$, agreeing with the pictured normal, so we may use the formula directly.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/46/4697accf510b0ea92f64022a1a6f75077d38bf2eed0273ca8a80cf8fb174567c.png)\n\nSubstitute the curve into both components of the field and differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t,&y&=t^2,\\\\\nP&=y=t^2,&Q&=2x=2t,\\\\\nx'&=1,&y'&=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[(t^2)(2t)-(2t)(1)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(2t^3-2t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^4}{2}-t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac12-1=-\\frac12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722390 :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/78/78d4d59fb9396f20ffad120ba369e913382e1abec1043ef1dfd74f8843b2d90f.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x=y^2$ from $(0,0)$ to $(4,2)$, with $0\\le y\\le2$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j$ through $C$ with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nChoose $y=t$, so $x=t^2$ and $0\\le t\\le2$. This parameterization starts at $(0,0)$ and ends at $(4,2)$. Its tangent direction is $(2t,1)$; rotating clockwise gives $(1,-2t)$, agreeing with the pictured normal.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/cb/cb18160c39c739e438aa05ffa1940e5feea9a1edfe9b9d46e177009735c9b8fb.png)\n\nAlong this parameterization,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t^2,&y&=t,\\\\\nP&=y=t,&Q&=x=t^2,\\\\\nx'&=2t,&y'&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^2\\left[(t)(1)-(t^2)(2t)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2(t-2t^3)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{2}-\\frac{t^4}{2}\\right]_0^2\\\\\n&=2-8=-6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722391 :question/problem "![Curve and chosen unit normal](images/38/3887a4f937c460a8a12ac1570f7f2763986792c2d7b3c627cf441a7fdcbd75ff.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf F(x,y)=2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$ through the line segment $C$ parameterized by $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2t\\,\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$, with respect to the unit normal $\\mathbf n$ shown.\n\n*Hint:* When the normal is clockwise from the tangent for increasing $t$, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ and a parameterization $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$, evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the curve. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_a^b\\left(Py'-Qx'\\right)\\mathrm dt,\n$$\n\nprovided $\\mathbf n$ is the $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent for increasing $t$.\n\nFor increasing $t$, the curve runs from $(0,0)$ to $(1,2)$. Its tangent points in the direction $(1,2)$; a clockwise rotation gives $(2,-1)$, which points toward the pictured normal. Thus the formula has the required sign.\n\n![Tangent and clockwise normal](images/20/2077b2f3007086a0d96cbbded142e8d07856dc7a5f331dbffb54b977bdac5c72.png)\n\nAlong $C$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=t,&y&=2t,\\\\\nP&=2,&Q&=1,\\\\\nx'&=1,&y'&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_0^1\\left[2(2)-1(1)\\right]\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 3\\,\\mathrm dt=[3t]_0^1=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sign is positive because the field has a positive component in the chosen normal direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722392 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b17d9c57364b57f5a41d1ef1eba46aefb14501d4e8e6ced0fc976bc1b6075c7c.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\pi)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $6$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\pi+\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}=\\frac{6\\pi}{6}+\\frac{5\\pi}{6}=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{11 \\pi}{6})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=6\\cos\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{3},\\\\y &=6\\sin\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)=6\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3 \\sqrt{3},-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722402 :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c9a038c2ca74c36cb49b3677b4ba6b80e4ed7fc7d0899eb828a4d6ecbace2e0.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3 \\sqrt{2},\\frac{5 \\pi}{6})$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3 \\sqrt{2}$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}-\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}=\\frac{10\\pi}{12}-\\frac{7\\pi}{12}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3 \\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=3,\\\\y &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=3 \\sqrt{2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722407 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4de04f48cc9db6c90301b15849b0634a6952500f3275372a4aa14b14ad739116.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(7,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{13 \\pi}{12}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $7$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+\\frac{13 \\pi}{12}=\\frac{3\\pi}{12}+\\frac{13\\pi}{12}=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(7,\\frac{4 \\pi}{3})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=7\\cos\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=7\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{7}{2},\\\\y &=7\\sin\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)=7\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(- \\frac{7}{2},- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722409 :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccf554ba2d8974f9ef26b9dcf1e32cbae1c67cbc46fdd055350c7e732d6ef81d.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{\\pi}{12}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{\\pi}{12}=\\frac{8\\pi}{12}+\\frac{1\\pi}{12}=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3\\cos\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\\\y &=3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=3\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722416 :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b3bc54b961daa66f9d92b61e7d034b81f78cf09a4bc8a82045337ff35289c11.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{2 \\pi}{3})$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}$.\n\nDetermine the exact Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $5$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}-\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}=\\frac{8\\pi}{12}-\\frac{5\\pi}{12}=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\frac{\\pi}{4})$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=5\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=5\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\\\y &=5\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=5\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722427 :question/problem "![](images/33/33061766ae30a387c2c1f6cb6f43ed45adcd36fa7a8914b7d4eed393a7161660.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(3,135^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $3$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=135^\\circ+15^\\circ=150^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(3,150^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=3\\cos\\left(150^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\\\y &=3\\sin\\left(150^\\circ\\right)=3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722431 :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f4bb9378ef45d352ca4e07fe76847daf3bbb94f752d0622ec4d1ec4598f1b07.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(8,315^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is clockwise, so subtract the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $8$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=315^\\circ-15^\\circ=300^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,300^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=8\\cos\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=8\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=4,\\\\y &=8\\sin\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=8\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- 4 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,- 4 \\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722439 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4b681702f7102d387b8e7ff910d39aefd016b79df88b0ef01ee17a781b5b047.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,210^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=90^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is clockwise, so subtract the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $10$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=210^\\circ-90^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(10,120^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=10\\cos\\left(120^\\circ\\right)=10\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-5,\\\\y &=10\\sin\\left(120^\\circ\\right)=10\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=5 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5 \\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722440 :question/problem "![](images/19/19e401148d6a6b874fbad93a4db3afe1b6fde55105fcc7798363ff2cd5533bb7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(9,225^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=75^\\circ$.\n\nMoving from $A$ to $B$ along the marked arc is counterclockwise, so add the marked angle. Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ to find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $9$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=225^\\circ+75^\\circ=300^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(9,300^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=9\\cos\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9}{2},\\\\y &=9\\sin\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{3}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722455 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4a75632d599b85c5ac0c8c826a9e42891628ec7c97d8a3c2100119bf417a986.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,60^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=30^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $2$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=60^\\circ-30^\\circ=30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,30^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=2\\cos\\left(30^\\circ\\right)=2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{3},\\\\y &=2\\sin\\left(30^\\circ\\right)=2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722461 :question/problem "![](images/28/2879a21b84b281a2578aed1a687bfb864df7017bbb55eb87bcc735c722fd5f12.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(9,60^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=15^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $9$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is clockwise; subtract the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=60^\\circ-15^\\circ=45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(9,45^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=9\\cos\\left(45^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\\\y &=9\\sin\\left(45^\\circ\\right)=9\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9 \\sqrt{2}}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722467 :question/problem "![](images/19/19e401148d6a6b874fbad93a4db3afe1b6fde55105fcc7798363ff2cd5533bb7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,225^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=75^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $6$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=225^\\circ+75^\\circ=300^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(6,300^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=6\\cos\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=6\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=3,\\\\y &=6\\sin\\left(300^\\circ\\right)=6\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=- 3 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(3,- 3 \\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722473 :question/problem "![](images/79/7925e3d8e4c24e41ac260b9c773a02a3aec1f1e12d70550ed06b0ca28135084d.png)\n\nThe diagram shows $A$ and $B$ on a circle centered at the origin $O$. Point $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,60^\\circ)$ and the marked angle is $\\angle AOB=60^\\circ$.\n\nUse the marked arc and the positions of $A$ and $B$ to decide whether to increase or decrease the angle of $A$.\n\nWhich ordered pair gives the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Both points lie on the same circle, so $B$ has radius $4$. Along the marked arc, moving from $A$ to $B$ is counterclockwise; add the marked angle.\n\n$$\n\\theta_B=60^\\circ+60^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThus $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,120^\\circ)$. Using $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &=4\\cos\\left(120^\\circ\\right)=4\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-2,\\\\y &=4\\sin\\left(120^\\circ\\right)=4\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=2 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2 \\sqrt{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722476 :question/problem "Find the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 10 \\\\ y&=8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\\\ t&\\in\\left[\\frac{\\pi}{6},\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 10\\right) \\\\ &=8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=64(\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t) \\\\ &=64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $8\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=8.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t=8 t+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}}8\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[8 t\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{12}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{10 \\pi}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722479 :question/problem "![](images/75/7528bd6a6c7590d73e0d4446b0fcf78183876938fd857565ba9860faaaae2563.png)\n\nFind the exact arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=- \\frac{t^{3}}{3} + t \\\\ y&=t^{2} \\\\ t&\\in\\left[0,3\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length is the integral of the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(x\\prime(t))^2+(y\\prime(t))^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{t^{3}}{3} + t\\right) \\\\ &=1 - t^{2} \\\\ y\\prime(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2&=\\left(1 - t^{2}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(2 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=t^{4} + 2 t^{2} + 1 \\\\ &=\\left(t^{2} + 1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $t^{2} + 1\\geq0$, so taking the square root gives\n\n$$\nv(t)=\\sqrt{(x\\prime)^2+(y\\prime)^2}=t^{2} + 1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int t^{2} + 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\\frac{t^{3}}{3} + t+C.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at both endpoints and subtract:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{3}t^{2} + 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{t^{3}}{3} + t\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left(12\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
